@startuml 13-single-writer-pattern
!include style.iuml

skinparam linetype ortho

package "Request path — N concurrent goroutines" {
  component "handler /v1/decide #1" as h1
  component "handler /v1/decide #2" as h2
  component "handler /v1/decide #N" as hN
  component "handler /v1/audit\n(dashboard, synchronous)" as ha
}

queue "buffered channel\nOS_AUDIT_BUFFER = 4096" as buf

package "Writer — exactly one goroutine" {
  component "Writer.run()\n**head : string**\nonly this goroutine touches it" as run
}

database "PostgreSQL\naudit_log" as pg
queue "Redis" as redis

h1 --> buf : Record() — never blocks
h2 --> buf : Record()
hN --> buf : Record()
ha --> buf : RecordSync() — waits for durability

buf --> run : strictly sequential consumption
run --> pg : 1. NormalizePayload\n2. Seal(head)\n3. INSERT
pg --> run : ok → head = entry.Hash
run --> redis : PUBLISH

note right of buf
  Queue full → the entry is **dropped and counted**.
  A request is never blocked: turning an audit backlog
  into an outage of the protected site would invert
  the system's purpose. The "dropped" counter is
  visible at /api/v1/status.
end note

note right of run
  **The invariant.** Each entry's hash covers the previous
  one, so appends must happen in strict order. Two
  goroutines sealing against the same head would produce
  two branches that no longer verify — a forked chain is
  indistinguishable from a tampered one.
  ..
  This is also why the dashboard does **not** write to the
  chain: it calls the engine's POST /v1/audit, which ends
  up in this same writer.
end note

note bottom of pg
  If the INSERT fails, head is left untouched: the next
  entry links to the last one that actually reached the
  store, and the chain stays continuous instead of
  pointing at an entry that does not exist.
end note

@enduml
